---
import ExamplePlan from '../../components/content/ExamplePlan.astro';
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';
import { examplesFor } from '../../lib/guides/examples';
import { feetInches, len, range, viewingDistanceFeet } from '../../lib/guides/measure';
import { withBase } from '../../lib/paths';

const slug = 'living-room-layout';

const meters = (feet: number) => (feet * 0.3048).toFixed(1);
const tvs = [43, 50, 55, 65, 75, 85].map((d) => ({
  size: d,
  near: viewingDistanceFeet(d, 40),
  far: viewingDistanceFeet(d, 30),
}));
---

<Guide slug={slug}>
  <p class="text-lg text-slate-600">
    A living room has to work for sitting, talking, watching and walking through. Most layouts that
    feel right follow a handful of distances: how far the coffee table is from the sofa, how wide
    the walkways are, and how far the seats are from the TV. Here they are, with example floor plans
    you can open and edit.
  </p>

  <h2 id="spacing">Spacing rules of thumb</h2>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Distance</th>
          <th scope="col">Typical range</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Sofa (or chair) to coffee table</th>
          <td>{range(14, 18)}</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Main walkway through the room</th>
          <td>{range(30, 36)}</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Narrow path between pieces, used now and then</th>
          <td>at least {len(24)}</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Coffee table length</th>
          <td>about two-thirds of the sofa's length</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Seats facing each other, for easy conversation</th>
          <td>no more than about 8–10 ft (2.4–3 m) apart</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Plan the walkways before the furniture: a path from each door to the seats, and through the room
    to other doors, should not run between the sofa and the coffee table.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tv-distance">TV viewing distance by screen size</h2>
  <p>
    Viewing distance is usually set by how much of your view the screen fills. SMPTE recommends at
    least 30° for general viewing; THX recommends about 40° for a cinema-like picture. For a 16:9
    screen, that works out to:
  </p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Screen (diagonal)</th>
          <th scope="col">Closer: 40° (THX)</th>
          <th scope="col">Farther: 30° (SMPTE)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {tvs.map((t) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">{t.size} in</th>
            <td>
              {feetInches(t.near)} <span class="whitespace-nowrap">({meters(t.near)} m)</span>
            </td>
            <td>
              {feetInches(t.far)} <span class="whitespace-nowrap">({meters(t.far)} m)</span>
            </td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="text-sm text-slate-600">
    Distances are from your eyes to the screen. Anywhere between the two columns is reasonable; sit
    closer for films, farther if the TV is mostly on in the background.
  </p>

  <h2 id="furniture-sizes">Typical living room furniture sizes</h2>
  <p>Sizes vary a lot between models, so measure yours or check the product page.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Three-seat sofa:</strong> about {range(72, 90)} wide, {range(32, 40)} deep.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Loveseat:</strong> about {range(52, 72)} wide, the same depth as a sofa.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Armchair:</strong> about {range(28, 40)} wide and deep.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Coffee table:</strong> about {range(36, 54)} long, {range(18, 30)} wide.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>TV console:</strong> a little wider than the TV, about {range(16, 20)} deep.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="tips">Layout tips</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Pick a focal point</strong> (TV, fireplace or window) and face the main seating
      towards it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Float the sofa</strong> away from the wall if a walkway would otherwise cut between
      the sofa and the coffee table. A sofa back makes a natural edge for the walkway.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Avoid glare:</strong> a TV directly opposite a bright window is hard to watch in the
      day. A side wall usually works better.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Leave every doorway clear.</strong> The planner marks a piece that stands in a doorway
      or in a door's swing.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="examples">Example living room layouts</h2>
  {examplesFor(slug).map((e) => (
    <ExamplePlan example={e} />
  ))}

  <p>
    Next: <a href={withBase('guides/home-office-layout/')}>home office layouts</a>, or the{' '}
    <a href={withBase('guides/bedroom-layout/')}>bedroom layout guide</a>.
  </p>
</Guide>
